# The cursor and player coordinate readout on the world map and under the minimap.
# Reads ns.L at FILE SCOPE, so every locale must precede it.
Modules\MapCoords.lua

Modules\History\Recorder.lua
Modules\History\Frame.lua
Modules\History\Session.lua

# Data\QuestCoords_Classic.lua is Era quest data and no retail map uses those UiMapIDs, so it
# is listed by the flavor TOCs ONLY. Both flavor TOCs are tracked now, so CI sees them and their
# own listings satisfy coverage. The directive stays as the declaration of record, and
# tools/check_toc.py errors if it goes stale.
# check-toc: flavor-only Data/QuestCoords_Classic.lua

# Data\QuestSpawns_Classic.lua is the objective SPAWN map for the same flavors - every place a
# quest can be advanced, rather than the one waypoint QuestCoords carries. Same reasoning, same
# gate, and it defines a DIFFERENT global so the two load together rather than overwriting.
# check-toc: flavor-only Data/QuestSpawns_Classic.lua

# Data\QuestTurnIn_Classic.lua is where each quest is HANDED IN, for the same flavors. Neither
# table above can answer that - QuestSpawns holds objective locations and QuestCoords' single
# point IS the objective for any quest that has one, so a finished quest was being pinned at the
# field it was farmed in. Retail gets the turn-in from Blizzard's own waypoint.
# check-toc: flavor-only Data/QuestTurnIn_Classic.lua

# Modules\Minimap\QuestPins.lua draws the objective pins on the minimap and needs
# HereBeDragons-Pins to do it. That library calls WorldMapFrame:AddDataProvider at FILE SCOPE, on
# the REAL canvas, which is what LibMapPinHandler's shadow canvas exists to keep EQ off on retail
# where the AreaPOI taint crash is live. So the library and its consumer are listed by the flavor
# TOCs only. Retail safety rests on this file not being listed here, NOT on LibStub answering
# nil - any retail user running another addon that embeds HereBeDragons has it loaded already.
# check-toc: flavor-only Modules/Minimap/QuestPins.lua

# Data\QuestNPCs_Classic.lua answers "which creatures advance this quest", which is what a
# nameplate icon needs and what no Classic API reports - a unit tooltip there carries no quest
# data at all, measured. Retail reads that from the tooltip and never loads this.
# check-toc: flavor-only Data/QuestNPCs_Classic.lua

# Data\QuestAvailable_Classic.lua answers "where can this quest be picked up, and may this
# character pick it up" - the one question the client cannot answer for a quest that is not in the
# log. Retail draws Blizzard's own markers for unaccepted quests and needs none of it.
# check-toc: flavor-only Data/QuestAvailable_Classic.lua

# Data\QuestCategory_Classic.lua says what KIND each quest is, which drives the map's category
# filters. Retail reads the same thing live from GetQuestTagInfo, so it needs none of it.
# check-toc: flavor-only Data/QuestCategory_Classic.lua

# Modules\MapPOI\Available.lua is the only consumer of that table. Listed by the flavor TOCs only
# for the same reason, and it self-gates on the table's presence as well.
# check-toc: flavor-only Modules/MapPOI/Available.lua

# The Quest Browser looks a quest up in the tables above, which is the only place that answer
# exists on Classic. Retail can already open any quest in Blizzard's own quest log and map, so
# there is nothing here for it to add and no data for it to read.
# check-toc: flavor-only Modules/QuestBrowser/Data.lua
# check-toc: flavor-only Modules/QuestBrowser/Frame.lua

# The six files below are the TBC dataset. They define exactly the same globals as the six
# _Classic files above and are ALTERNATIVES to them, never companions - EverythingQuests_TBC.toc
# lists these and EverythingQuests_Vanilla.toc lists those, so no client ever loads both and no
# global is ever written twice. TBC quest data is a superset of Era's, which is why TBC gets its
# own set rather than reusing the Era files: those carry nothing for Outland or for the Blood Elf
# and Draenei starting zones.
# check-toc: flavor-only Data/QuestCoords_TBC.lua
# check-toc: flavor-only Data/QuestSpawns_TBC.lua
# check-toc: flavor-only Data/QuestTurnIn_TBC.lua
# check-toc: flavor-only Data/QuestNPCs_TBC.lua
# check-toc: flavor-only Data/QuestAvailable_TBC.lua
# check-toc: flavor-only Data/QuestCategory_TBC.lua

